my name is Daniel : Ramos I am a cell  biologist at Yale University and my lab  in collaboration with Herreshoff at the  NIH and sear on Bao at sloan-kettering  recently developed a new very powerful  imaging technology that allows to  visualize with single-cell resolution  the development of an organism in its  entirety  this technology is called inverted  selective plane illumination microscopy  and is 30 times faster than the fastest  microscopes that are conventionally used  in the field we use a worm a nematode  that's called C elegans as a model  organism in this movie you're seeing an  embryo of C elegans as it's developing  with a conventional microscope we will  have to expose this embryo for 14 hours  of continuous illumination and that will  kill the animal because it will receive  too much light and it will burn our  microscope is much faster and exposes  the animal to less light therefore we  can visualize the entire development of  the animal without causing harm  moreover the speed of this microscope  now allows us to see events that were  never seen before for example in this  embryo we can actually visualize the  position of all cells you can see the  nuclei in red and then in green you're  seeing the positions of neurons those  neurons now we can tell where they're  born and where they're going where  they're gonna form connections where  they're gonna where their final  destination is going to be that allows  us to build an atlas of development for  this animal the atlas will be enormously  useful we really don't understand how  genes regulate how neurons come together  as a system in a whole animal to  assemble into circuits and regulate  behaviors genes are conserved through  our evolution if we were to understand  how genes are regulating this process in  the Atlas this will have huge  implications for research and it will  also influence the way that we think  about every disease imaginable
 

